http: accept a reason phrase glued to the status code

e48a073d7e (0136) requires whitespace or the end of the line after the
three digits of the status code, as RFC 9112 does. IPTV panels and cheap
middleboxes answer "HTTP/1.1 200OK" (some Xtream-codes forks): before
0136 that parsed as code 200 and played; with it every channel of such a
provider fails the open with "Malformed HTTP status code".

Accept a letter after the code too: the reason starts there. A fourth
digit ("2000"), a letter inside the code ("20x") and a sign or other
character after it ("200+OK") still fail. Letters are tested without the
locale (libavutil has no av_isalpha).

diff --git a/libavformat/http.c b/libavformat/http.c
index c805ffa..575f915 100644
--- a/libavformat/http.c
+++ b/libavformat/http.c
@@ -1059,9 +1059,12 @@ int ff_http_parse_status_line(void *logctx, const char *line, HTTPStatusLine *st
         p++;
 
     /* RFC 9112 mandates a space after the code, but a bare "HTTP/1.1 200"
-     * is common enough in the wild to be worth accepting. */
+     * is common enough in the wild to be worth accepting, and so is a
+     * reason glued to the code ("HTTP/1.1 200OK", IPTV panels): a fourth
+     * digit or a sign is still no code. */
     if (!av_isdigit(p[0]) || !av_isdigit(p[1]) || !av_isdigit(p[2]) ||
-        (p[3] && !av_isspace(p[3]))) {
+        (p[3] && !av_isspace(p[3]) &&
+         !(av_toupper(p[3]) >= 'A' && av_toupper(p[3]) <= 'Z'))) {
         av_log(logctx, AV_LOG_ERROR, "Malformed HTTP status code.\n");
         return AVERROR_INVALIDDATA;
     }
